                <head>412. Paper Prepared in the Central Intelligence Agency<anchor n="1"
                        xml:id="d412-f1"/></head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">
                        <idno type="document-identifier">ALA M 88–20036</idno>
                    </seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>Washington</placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1988-05-06">May 6, 1988</date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <pb n="1" type="facsimile"/>
                <p rendition="#section-heading #italic">Paraguay: Expanding Illegal International
                    Activities</p>
                <p rendition="#section-heading #italic">Summary</p>
                <p>President Stroessner has deliberately fostered institutionalized corruption in
                    Paraguay to help preserve his authoritarian control over the country.
                    Corruption, which extends into every level of the bureaucracy, has facilitated
                    the growth of a thriving underground economy. We believe, however, that
                    Paraguay’s lawless atmosphere is now encouraging more ominous international
                    illegal activities—involving passport sales, arms transfers, technology
                    transfers, and counterfeit US currency—often with a narcotics connection. These
                    operations are increasingly bringing together corrupt local officials and
                    fugitive foreign criminals, and we believe that recent reporting only scratches
                    the surface of the problem.</p>
                <p>[Omitted here is the body of the paper.]</p>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
